 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Do Ravens Recognize Their Owner? Individual Recognition in Ravens';
const description = 'Ravens can recognise individual humans by face and voice, and form strong bonds with specific people. This page examines what science says about raven individual recognition, including recognition of their carers and of human "enemies."';
const path        = '/questions/do-ravens-recognize-their-owner/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Do Ravens Recognize Their Owner?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Can ravens recognize individual humans?', a: 'Yes. Ravens (and closely related crows) are well-documented face-recognisers. Wild ravens show differential responses to different humans — approaching some individuals and avoiding others based on past interactions. Research on crows (Marzluff et al., 2008, Animal Cognition) demonstrated that crows can remember a specific threatening human face and alter their behaviour toward that person for years, suggesting ravens have similar abilities. Captive ravens strongly distinguish their regular carers from strangers.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens form bonds with humans?', a: 'Yes, particularly in captivity. Ravens that have been raised by or regularly interact with specific humans show individual recognition, approach their carers selectively, engage in play and social behaviour with them, and can form lasting bonds. Konrad Lorenz described in detail the behaviour of ravens he raised, including their apparent attachment to him as an individual. Wild ravens in areas with regular human contact (Tower of London ravens, research facility birds) similarly show strong individual recognition.' },
  { q: 'How long can ravens remember a person?', a: 'The duration of individual recognition in ravens has not been precisely measured. In closely related crows (Corvus brachyrhynchos), Marzluff et al. showed that recognition of a specific human face persisted for at least 2 years without reinforcement. Given that ravens have similar or greater cognitive capacities to crows, long-term retention of individual human faces (years) is plausible. The long lifespan of ravens (10–20+ years wild) suggests they could retain such recognition for extended periods.' },
];
---

